<exercise number="">
        <title>Question 1.C</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Consider the following boundary value problems and identify if the shooting method and/or the finite differences method can be used to solve the given problem. If neither method can be used then please state neither and also explain your answer.</p>
            <ol>
                <li><p><m>y'' + y' - 2y = t^2</m>; <m>\quad y(0)=4 \text{ and } y(3)=-1</m> </p></li>
                <li><p><m>y'' + y' - 2y = t^2</m>; <m>\quad y'(0)=1 \text{ and } y'(3)=-2</m> </p></li>
                <li><p><m>y'' + y'y = \sin(t)</m>; <m>\quad y(0)=3 \text{ and } y(4)= 1</m></p></li>
                <li><p><m>y'' + y'y = \sin(t)</m>; <m>\quad y(0)=3 \text{ and } y'(4)= 1</m> </p></li>
                <li><p><m>y'' + y'y = \sin(t)</m>; <m>\quad y'(0)=3 \text{ and } y'(4)= 1</m> </p></li>
                <li><p><m>y'' + 2 y' + 6 y = e^t</m>; <m>\quad y(1)=2 \text{ and } y(5)= -1</m> </p></li>
                <li><p><m>y''' + 2 y'' + 6 y = e^t</m>; <m>\quad y'(0)=2 \text{ and } y(5)= -1</m> </p></li>
                <li><p><m>y'' + 2 y'y = e^{-t}</m>; <m>\quad y'(0)=2 \text{ and } y'(1)= -1</m> </p></li>
                <li><p><m>y'' + 2 y'y = e^{-t}</m>; <m>\quad y(0)=2 \text{ and } y(1)= -1</m> </p></li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <ol>
             <li><p><m>y'' + y' - 2y = t^2</m>; <m>\quad y(0)=4 \text{ and } y(3)=-1</m> <alert>[both]</alert></p></li>
                <li><p><m>y'' + y' - 2y = t^2</m>; <m>\quad y'(0)=1 \text{ and } y'(3)=-2</m> <alert>[finite differences]</alert></p></li>
                <li><p><m>y'' + y'y = \sin(t)</m>; <m>\quad y(0)=3 \text{ and } y(4)= 1</m> <alert>[shooting]</alert></p></li>
                <li><p><m>y'' + y'y = \sin(t)</m>; <m>\quad y(0)=3 \text{ and } y'(4)= 1</m> <alert>[neither (could adapt shooting)]</alert></p></li>
                <li><p><m>y'' + y'y = \sin(t)</m>; <m>\quad y'(0)=3 \text{ and } y'(4)= 1</m> <alert>[neither]</alert></p></li>
                <li><p><m>y'' + 2 y' + 6 y = e^t</m>; <m>\quad y(1)=2 \text{ and } y(5)= -1</m> <alert>[both]</alert></p></li>
                <li><p><m>y''' + 2 y'' + 6 y = e^t</m>; <m>\quad y'(0)=2 \text{ and } y(5)= -1</m> <alert>[neither]</alert></p></li>
                <li><p><m>y'' + 2 y'y = e^{-t}</m>; <m>\quad y'(0)=2 \text{ and } y'(1)= -1</m> <alert>[neither]</alert></p></li>
                <li><p><m>y'' + 2 y'y = e^{-t}</m>; <m>\quad y(0)=2 \text{ and } y(1)= -1</m> <alert>[shooting]</alert></p></li>
                
</ol>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 1.D</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Use finite difference using the centered difference approximation for each derivative for the following BVP:
                <me>y'' + 2y' + 6y = t^2 + 1, \quad y'(0)=1, \text{ and } y'(2) = 2.</me>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <me>
                \begin{pmatrix} 
                (6h^2-h-1) \amp (1+h) \amp \amp 0 \\ 
                (1-h) \amp (6h^2-2) \amp (1+h) \amp \\ 
                \ddots \amp \ddots \amp \ddots \amp \\ 
                \amp (1-h) \amp (6h^2-2) \amp (1+h) \\ 
                0 \amp \amp (1-h) \amp (6h^2+h-1) 
                \end{pmatrix} 
                \begin{pmatrix} y_1 \\ y_2 \\ \vdots \\ \vdots \\ y_{n-2} \\ y_{n-1}\end{pmatrix} 
                = \begin{pmatrix} h^2 (t_1^2+1)+h(1-h) \\ h^2(t_2^2+1) \\ \vdots \\ \vdots \\ h^2(t_{n-2}^2+1) \\ h^2(t_{n-1}^2+1)-2h(1+h)\end{pmatrix}
            </me>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 1.E</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Use finite difference using the centered difference approximation for the second derivative and the forward difference for the first derivative for the following BVP:
                <me>y'' - 3y' + 6y = t^2 + 1, \quad y(0)=3, \text{ and } y(1) = 5.</me>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <me>
                \begin{pmatrix} 
                (6h^2+3h-2) \amp (1-3h) \amp \amp 0 \\ 
                1 \amp (6h^2+3h-2) \amp (1-3h) \amp \\ 
                \ddots \amp \ddots \amp \ddots \amp \\ 
                \amp 1 \amp (6h^2+3h-2) \amp (1-3h) \\ 
                0 \amp \amp 1 \amp (6h^2+3h-2) 
                \end{pmatrix} 
                \begin{pmatrix} y_1 \\ y_2 \\ \vdots \\ \vdots \\ y_{n-2} \\ y_{n-1}\end{pmatrix} 
                = \begin{pmatrix} h^2 (t_1^2+1)-3 \\ h^2(t_2^2+1) \\ \vdots \\ \vdots \\ h^2(t_{n-2}^2+1) \\ h^2(t_{n-1}^2+1)-5(1-3h)\end{pmatrix}
            </me>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 1.F</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Use Matlab to solve the problems in 1.D and 1.E using the step sizes of 0.1 and 0.05. Can shooting method be used to solve either 1.D or 1.E?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>We will illustrate using the technique given in the lecture notes. The work to follow is for 1.D using <m>h = 0.1</m>:</p>
            <program language="matlab">
                <input><![CDATA[
                % initialize x
                >> h = 0.1; x = 0:h:2; x = x'; n = length(x);
                % This includes endpoints of x=0 and x=2 so the length we want is n-2
                % First build D
                >> D = diag((6*h.^2-2)*ones(n-2,1),0);
                % need to fix the first and last entry due to boundary conditions
                >> D(1,1) = 6*h.^2-h-1; D(n-2,n-2) = 6*h.^2+h-1;
                >> Dsup = diag((1+h)*ones(n-3,1),1);
                >> Dsub = diag((1-h)*ones(n-3,1),-1);
                >> A = D+Dsup+Dsub;
                % To start the right hand side we consider h^2 *(x^2+1)
                >> b = h.^2*(x(2:n-1).^2+1);
                % The next part is to fix the first and last elements of the right hand side 
                % to accommodate the boundary conditions. 
                >> b(1) = b(1) + h*(1-h);
                >> b(n-2)= b(n-2)-2*h*(1+h);
                % The linear system is complete and we can solve. 
                >> y = A\b;
                % We can plot the solution. 
                >> plot(x(2:n-1),y)
                ]]></input>
            </program>
            <p>The same work can be repeated for <m>h=0.05</m> and similar work can be done to apply Matlab to solve 1.E. Shooting Method cannot be used on 1.D, but shooting method could be used on 1.E.</p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 1.G</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Formulate the matrix solution to 1.E using centered difference for the second derivative and backward difference for the first derivative.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <me>
                \begin{pmatrix} 
                (6h^2-3h-2) \amp 1 \amp \amp 0 \\ 
                (1+3h) \amp (6h^2-3h-2) \amp 1 \amp \\ 
                \ddots \amp \ddots \amp \ddots \amp \\ 
                \amp (1+3h) \amp (6h^2-3h-2) \amp 1 \\ 
                0 \amp \amp (1+3h) \amp (6h^2-3h-2) 
                \end{pmatrix} 
                \begin{pmatrix} y_1 \\ y_2 \\ \vdots \\ \vdots \\ y_{n-2} \\ y_{n-1}\end{pmatrix} 
                = \begin{pmatrix} h^2 (t_1^2+1)-3(1+3h) \\ h^2(t_2^2+1) \\ \vdots \\ \vdots \\ h^2(t_{n-2}^2+1) \\ h^2(t_{n-1}^2+1)-5\end{pmatrix}
            </me>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    
