            <p>
                <alert>Section 3: Numerical Techniques for Solving Linear Systems</alert>
            </p>
      
    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 3.A</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Describe what is meant with Gaussian Elimination with partial pivoting and discuss why partial pivoting would be necessary.</p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 3.B</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Define the condition number for a matrix and explain what the implications of a condition number might mean for solving a linear system. Is it desirable to have a small or large condition number?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>The condition number can be estimated by taking the ratio of the magnitude of the largest eigenvalue (largest in magnitude) for a matrix over the magnitude of the smallest eigenvalue (smallest in magnitude) for a matrix. Thus, a singular matrix would have an infinite condition number since a singular matrix has an eigenvalue equal to zero; officially we should say the condition number is not defined for singular matrices. For a nonsingular matrix, the condition number is always positive and it is always greater than, or equal to, one. The smaller the condition number is, the better conditioned it is and the better behaved numerical methods are for solving a well conditioned system. If the condition number is large then it is known as ill-conditioned and these systems can present challenges to numerical solvers.</p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 3.C</title>
        <statement>
            <p>What are the advantages to using the Matlab function of <c>A\b</c> to solve a linear system instead of using <m>A^{-1}b</m>? Please be as complete as possible in your answer. Are there any circumstances where it is more advantageous to use <m>A^{-1}</m>?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>The function of <c>A\b</c> includes computational procedures that make the calculation more stable and more accurate. The use of pivoting would be a simplistic analogy for the kinds of things <c>A\b</c> does to help make the solution more stable and more accurate. In addition, <c>A\b</c> includes elements to speed up the calculations since calculating the full inverse of a matrix is very expensive to accomplish computationally.</p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

