    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 4.D</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Write a Matlab program that implements the Power Method given a matrix A, an initial vector v, and a number iterations to run the method, n.</p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 4.E</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Describe a method to design an automatic stopping condition for the Power Method based on convergence.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Recall that the coefficients of <m>c_k</m> in the method converge to the largest eigenvalue in absolute value. Thus, a stopping condition of <m>|c_{k-1} - c_k| \lt \text{tol}</m> is a way to stop the algorithm based on convergence.</p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

  <p>
                <alert>Section 5: Extra Questions</alert>
     </p>   

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 5.A</title>
        <statement>
            <p>State the definition of local truncation error for solving a differential system:
                <me>\frac{d\vec{w}}{dt} = \vec{F}(t,\vec{w}), \quad \vec{w}(0)=\vec{w}_0.</me>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <me>\text{LTE}=\frac{\Vert \vec{w}(t_1)-\vec{w}_1 \Vert}{\Delta t}</me>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 5.B</title>
        <statement>
            <p>State the definition for order of convergence for a numerical method solving a system. In this case, the output of the method involves vectors, <m>\vec{w}_k</m> converging to a solution <m>\vec{w}</m>, i.e., <m>\vec{w}_k \rightarrow \vec{w}</m> as <m>k\rightarrow \infty</m>.</p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 5.C</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Explain the link between finding eigenvalues and the root finding methods that were covered early in the course.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>The explanation is really in the definition. Eigenvalues are roots to the characteristic equation, i.e. eigenvalues are solutions to the equation <m>\det(A - \lambda I) = 0</m>.</p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 5.D</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Use Matlab to compute the condition number for the system:</p>
            <md>
                <mrow>x + y + z \amp= 2</mrow>
                <mrow>x + 2z \amp= 1</mrow>
                <mrow>2x + y + 3.0001z \amp= 3.0001</mrow>
            </md>
            <p>Interpret what the condition number might mean for solving this system.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p> In MATLAB we have </p>
            <program>
                <input>
            >> A = [1 1 1;1 0 2; 2 1 3.001];
            >> cond(A)
            ans =1.9516e+04
</input> </program> 
<p> There isn't a clear line for when a condition number is too large. Notice that if we replace the 3.001 entry with 3 then the matrix becomes singular so this matrix is near singular and could be challenging to solve due to the large condition number. </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>


